WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.506
Authorization is often presented as a
Boolean question,

2
00:00:03.586 --> 00:00:06.467
but production evaluation has more runtime
outcomes.

3
00:00:06.547 --> 00:00:10.242
A policy can allow or deny, yet the
application can also lack identity,

4
00:00:10.322 --> 00:00:12.765
tenant, resource ownership, or object
state.

5
00:00:12.845 --> 00:00:15.475
A database can fail, a policy service can
time out,

6
00:00:15.555 --> 00:00:19.000
configuration can be malformed, or code
can throw unexpectedly.

7
00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:23.026
The security design must account for every
path that reaches the protected

8
00:00:23.106 --> 00:00:23.732
operation.

9
00:00:23.812 --> 00:00:27.820
A variable initialized to true before an
asynchronous policy call creates a

10
00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:28.777
fail-open path.

11
00:00:28.857 --> 00:00:33.116
If the call throws and a catch block
continues, the permissive default survives.

12
00:00:33.196 --> 00:00:37.016
Similar defects appear in comparisons such
as result not equal to false,

13
00:00:37.096 --> 00:00:39.789
or in a fallback that replaces undefined
with true.

14
00:00:39.869 --> 00:00:43.940
These patterns treat absence of a denial
as permission even though no trusted

15
00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:46.275
component established an allow decision.

16
00:00:46.355 --> 00:00:50.300
Authorization must complete before any
irreversible side effect begins;

17
00:00:50.380 --> 00:00:54.201
a later denial cannot reliably undo an
external call or emitted message.

18
00:00:54.281 --> 00:00:57.224
Start protected operations in a
non-authorized state.

19
00:00:57.304 --> 00:01:01.250
Proceed only after the applicable policy
returns an explicit allow for the

20
00:01:01.330 --> 00:01:04.837
authenticated subject, resource, action,
and relevant context.

21
00:01:04.917 --> 00:01:09.051
A Boolean policy function can be
appropriate when false clearly means deny and

22
00:01:09.131 --> 00:01:11.511
exceptions propagate into a non-grant
path.

23
00:01:11.591 --> 00:01:15.724
More complex systems often benefit from
representing deny and indeterminate

24
00:01:15.804 --> 00:01:17.934
separately so failures remain visible.

25
00:01:18.014 --> 00:01:22.022
A real policy denial and an evaluation
failure share one security property:

26
00:01:22.102 --> 00:01:24.294
the protected side effect does not occur.

27
00:01:24.374 --> 00:01:27.004
They need not share the same operational
meaning.

28
00:01:27.084 --> 00:01:31.218
A denial may map to a forbidden or
not-found response according to application

29
00:01:31.298 --> 00:01:35.494
policy, while an unavailable dependency
may produce a service-unavailable or

30
00:01:35.574 --> 00:01:37.015
internal-error response.

31
00:01:37.095 --> 00:01:39.537
Logging and metrics should distinguish
them.

32
00:01:39.617 --> 00:01:44.126
Fail-secure authorization means permission
requires positive, trusted evidence;

33
00:01:44.206 --> 00:01:48.340
it does not mean every technical failure
must be mislabeled as a policy denial.
